\cf0 Harada, T., et al. "Real time control of 3D sound space by gesture."\
\pard\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\ri-340\partightenfactor0
\cf0 	In most interactive performance systems, the output's tone and tempo are controlled in real time to allow for artistic improvisation. However, the supposed spatial layout of instruments is fixed during the performance. In the proposed system, the position and movement of the sound sources in 3D space, interactively, were controlled by the performer's natural gesture. The system consists of Data Glove, the gesture recognition system, the MIDI controller, and three speaker pairs placed oppositely on X, Y, and Z axes. Performers can use the proposed system to control the 3D sound interactively and thus integrate improvised sound motion into their performance. [Abstractor: authors]\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri-340\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
Hawley, K. and F. Macpherson (2011). \ul The admissible contents of experience\ulnone . Chichester, West Sussex, UK ; Malden, MA, Wiley-Blackwell.\
\pard\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\ri-340\partightenfactor0
\cf0 	"Which objects and properties are represented in perceptual experience? Although perceptual experiences frequently give rise to beliefs, the content of these beliefs do not always simply reflect the contents of the experiences on which they are based. Instead, they often rest on background knowledge and beliefs, as well as experience. This raises the question of how are we able to determine what the admissible contents of experience are, whether they include singular or existential contents, and whether they include contents pertaining to causation or natural kinds. The papers in this collection address these issues, together with questions concerning the nature of perceptual content. They deal with the central issues of whether perceptual content is similar to the content of the propositional attitudes; whether all states with content fall neatly into the categories of either belief or experience. The book also focuses on whether there exists a continuum from states that are more like perceptual experiences to states that are more like belief, and, indeed, ultimately whether we should consider perceptual experiences to have content at all. This ground-breaking volume is published in association with the journal Philosophical Quarterly"--\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri-340\partightenfactor0
